                <pb facs="0765" n="739" xml:id="pg739"/>

                <head>376. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On December 23, 1963, Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, General Maxwell Taylor, met with President Johnson at 2:45 p.m., primarily to discuss
                        Taylor’s recent visit to India and
                    Pakistan. At the end of the meeting, the President and Taylor briefly touched upon Vietnam. Taylor’s account of this discussion reads as
                    follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p><hi rendition="#italic">“Vietnam situation</hi>. He expressed concern
                            over the situation in Vietnam. Plainly, he had been impressed by
                            Secretary McNamara’s recent
                            report. The aspects of the discussion included personal consideration of
                                Lodge and Harkins. I ventured the opinion that
                                Harkins was probably the
                            only general who could get along with Lodge and that the real solution probably lay in the
                            substitution of a single military man to act both as Ambassador and as
                                COMUSMACV. I had in mind the
                            status of USCOB in Berlin.” (National Defense University, Taylor Papers,
                            T-36-71)</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>For McNamara’s report, see <ref
                        target="#d374">Document 374</ref></p>
            </div>
